{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>For the name of HaShem I call.</b> It refers to what precedes: 'Give ear, O heavens, and O earth' — you, the heavens and the earth and the four winds of the world, and all that is in you — you must know to give greatness and praise to HaShem, blessed be He, when I mention His name in the reading of the Torah. And thus it says: 'Praise Him, heavens and earth'; and it says: 'The heavens declare the glory of El' etc. — for through it you are sustained, as it is written: 'If not for My covenant day and night, I would not have appointed the ordinances of heaven and earth.' And by this name that I call, you were created, for it has four letters: yod, he, vav, he, and they form six words, corresponding to the four winds and the heavens and the earth, which were created by it." "<b>For the name of HaShem I call.</b> Translated in the Yerushalmi Targum: 'Said Moses the prophet: Woe to you, wicked ones, who mention the name of the Holy One — for it is impossible for one of the angels of the highest heights to mention the explicit name until the time that they say, קק״ק three times. And from them Moses learned, that he would not mention the explicit name until the time that he would prepare his mouth with twenty-one words, which are eighty-five letters.' The explanation: from 'האזינו' until the mention of 'ה׳' there are twenty-one words, consisting of eighty-five letters. And so it is in the Sifrei: 'Moses said: It is enough for me to be after seven angels.' The explanation: they mention the Name after three words — 'קדוש קדוש קדוש' — and it is enough for me after seven of them, meaning: to mention the Name after seven times three words, which are twenty-one words. And they are the words from 'האזינו' until the mention of the Name. Therefore the Sages enacted twenty-one words in the Kedushah recited every day before the mention of the Name. And this is their order: 'נקדש את שמך בעולם כשם שמקדישין אותו בשמי מרום וכן כתוב על יד נביאך וקרא זה אל זה ואמר קדוש קדוש קדוש ה׳' etc. And in the Kedushah of Yoṣer of Shabbat, eighty-five letters — and this is its detail: 'נקדישך ונעריצך כסוד שיח שרפי קדש המקדישים שמך בקדש ככתוב על יד נביאך וקרא זה אל זה ואמר קק״ק.' And likewise in Musaf we may not mention the Name before eighty-five letters, and this is the order of the reading and the detail of the writing: 'כתר יתנו לך המוני מעלה עם עמך קבוצי מטה יחד קדושה לך ישלשו ככתוב על יד נביאך וקרא זה אל זה ואמר קק״ק.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {1 "The abbreviation קק״ק stands for קדוש קדוש קדוש in the liturgical contexts cited. The Targum Yerushalmi passage is rendered as found in the Hebrew, preserving its Aramaic character within the translation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:55:07.066064423Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}